1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:03,740
You type a name, hit Enter, and there's the page.

2
00:00:03,670 --> 00:00:05,900
How does the internet know where to go?

3
00:00:05,830 --> 00:00:08,300
Thing is, it doesn't know names.

4
00:00:08,230 --> 00:00:11,500
It only knows numbers: IP addresses.

5
00:00:11,430 --> 00:00:14,140
So your phone asks DNS first.

6
00:00:14,070 --> 00:00:17,420
Like contacts: you tap a name, you're calling a number.

7
00:00:17,350 --> 00:00:20,540
If DNS doesn't know, it asks a chain.

8
00:00:20,470 --> 00:00:22,620
A root server points to dot org,

9
00:00:22,550 --> 00:00:24,940
dot org to Wikipedia's own server,

10
00:00:24,910 --> 00:00:26,860
and that one has the number.

11
00:00:26,750 --> 00:00:29,980
The answer's kept a few minutes, so next time's quick.

12
00:00:29,870 --> 00:00:33,420
Your request goes out as a packet, labelled with that number.

13
00:00:33,350 --> 00:00:35,660
No router knows the whole route.

14
00:00:35,630 --> 00:00:37,980
Each reads the number and passes it on.

15
00:00:37,910 --> 00:00:40,940
Router to router, under the sea from Australia,

16
00:00:40,830 --> 00:00:42,727
to a server in Singapore.

17
00:00:42,603 --> 00:00:44,540
The page comes back in packets,

18
00:00:44,590 --> 00:00:45,900
addressed to you.

19
00:00:45,870 --> 00:00:49,393
So: name to number, and the number finds the way.

20
00:00:49,297 --> 00:00:51,900
And it all happens again, every time
